= Solution
{parent=i}

**$A=[\mathrm{Co(NH_3)_5(OH)}]^{2+}$, $B=[\mathrm{Co(NH_3)_5(ONO)}]^{2+}$, $C=[\mathrm{Co(NH_3)_5(NO_2)}]^{2+}$, and the by-product is $\mathrm{HNO_2}$.** The first transformation is <deprotonation> of coordinated <water>, not loss of an <ammonia> ligand. The <hydroxo ligand> in $A$ is then nitrosated at <oxygen>, retaining its original Co–O attachment in the initially formed O-bound <nitrito ligand>. Atom and <electric charge> balance give
$$
[\mathrm{Co(NH_3)_5(OH)}]^{2+}+\mathrm{N_2O_3}
\longrightarrow[\mathrm{Co(NH_3)_5(ONO)}]^{2+}+\mathrm{HNO_2}.
$$
The <relative molecular mass> of <nitrous acid> is approximately $1+14+2(16)=47$. Heating converts the O-bound <nitrito ligand> to the N-bound <nitro ligand>; these are <linkage isomers>, with Co remaining in <oxidation state> $+3$ throughout.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The N-bound isomer $C$ is the thermodynamically favored form; heating accelerates access to it.** Formation of $B$ retains the original metal–<oxygen> attachment and is a plausible <kinetic control> outcome. Rearranging that attachment requires overcoming an <activation free energy>, especially at a substitutionally inert low-spin <cobalt>(III) center. Raising <temperature> speeds the <linkage isomerization>, allowing the more stable N-bound <nitro ligand> to accumulate. Its stronger <ligand field> and different covalent bonding help rationalize the stability, but a larger field splitting alone is not a complete calculation of the isomerization <Gibbs free energy>. The classical crystallographic comparison establishes the different attachments and thermodynamic direction.<Nitrito-Nitro Linkage Isomerization in the Solid State. 2. A Comparative Study of the Structures of Nitrito- and Nitropentaamminecobalt(III) Dichloride by Ingmar Grenthe and Ellika Nordin>[1979, pp. 1869–1874, structural comparison and solid-state isomerization]{ref}

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The relevant <spectrochemical series> order is O-bound nitrito $<$ <ammonia> $<$ N-bound nitro. A <pi-donor ligand> tends to raise the metal orbitals derived from $t_{2g}$, reducing the <octahedral splitting>; a <pi-acceptor ligand> stabilizes those orbitals, increasing the splitting. Since <photon energy> is $E=hc/\lambda$, the stronger-field complex absorbs at the shorter <wavelength>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\lambda_C=458\ \mathrm{nm},\qquad
\lambda_{[\mathrm{Co(NH_3)_6}]^{3+}}=474\ \mathrm{nm},\qquad
\lambda_B=486\ \mathrm{nm}.}
$$
The corresponding <spectroscopic wavenumbers> are approximately $21834$, $21097$ and $20576\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. These are the observed band energies, not three exact independent determinations of $\Delta_o$: low-spin $d^6$ transition energies also contain <electron repulsion> contributions, and replacing one ligand lowers the ideal octahedral <symmetry>. The relative assignment is nevertheless robust.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**$B$ removes more blue-green light and appears orange-red; $C$ removes shorter-wavelength blue light and appears yellow.** The observed <color> is the combined transmitted part of the visible spectrum, approximately complementary to the absorbed band, rather than the color corresponding to the absorption maximum itself. The longer-wavelength band of the O-bound <nitrito ligand> complex therefore shifts the transmitted balance toward orange/red compared with the N-bound <nitro ligand> complex. Band width, other absorption bands, <concentration> and optical path length affect the shade and darkness; a single maximum does not quantitatively determine the perceived <color>.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Both complexes contain <cobalt>(III), hence a $d^6$ metal ion. The approximately <octahedral molecular geometry>, relatively high metal <oxidation state> and five <ammonia> donors make the <octahedral splitting> large enough that pairing is favorable. Their low-spin configuration is
$$
\boxed{t_{2g}^{6}e_g^{0},\qquad S=0.}
$$
All six metal $d$ <electrons> are paired, and the <ligands in coordination chemistry> have closed shells, so neither complex has a permanent spin <magnetic moment>: both exhibit <diamagnetism>. The difference between N and O attachment is insufficient to change this low-spin assignment.

