Use C1 for the carbonyl <carbon>, C2 for the adjacent <carbon>, and C3 for the beta <carbon> of the reactive <enone> fragment $\mathrm{R{-}C1(=O){-}C2H{=}C3H{-}R'}$. **<water> adds conjugately at C3, giving a beta-hydroxy ketone.** The <oxygen> <lone pair> of neutral <water> attacks C3; move the C2=C3 pi pair toward C1–C2 and the carbonyl pi pair onto <oxygen>. This produces the charge-separated <enolate>–oxonium intermediate
$$
\mathrm{R{-}C1(O^-){=}C2H{-}C3H(OH_2^+){-}R'}.
$$
A second <water> molecule can relay a <proton> from the newly attached oxonium <oxygen> to the <enolate> system. Using the carbon-centered <resonance structure>, reform C1=O and protonate C2 while the donating O–H bond returns its pair to the added <oxygen>. Equivalently, <oxygen> protonation first gives an <enol>, followed by water-mediated <keto-enol tautomerism>. Either path yields a neutral beta-hydroxy ketone and regenerates the relay <water>. There is no need to introduce externally supplied H$^+$ or OH$^-$; solvent molecules perform the <proton> transfers. The uncatalyzed charge separation has a large barrier, so this mechanism describes a slow formal pathway, not a claim of rapid hydration in pure <water>.

\Image[paper-2-mechanisms.png]
{title=Reactive-fragment mechanisms for conjugate hydration, steroid reduction and finasteride trapping}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The C–O-forming transition state has a partial bond from <water> <oxygen> to C3, positive charge developing on that <oxygen>, and negative charge delocalizing toward the carbonyl <oxygen>.** A compact drawing is
$$
\left[\mathrm{R{-}C1(\overset{\delta-}{O})\cdots C2H\cdots C3H(R')\cdots\overset{\delta+}{OH_2}}\right]^\ddagger.
$$
Here the two C–C dotted connections denote redistribution of pi bonding, not cleavage of either <carbon>–<carbon> sigma bond. In a bond-order drawing retain the full sigma skeleton: C1–O is changing from double toward single, C1–C2 from single toward double, C2–C3 from double toward single, and C3–O from absent toward single. The incoming <water> approaches a face of the approximately planar conjugated unit and C3 begins to become tetrahedral. The total <electric charge> remains zero. If <proton> transfer to another <water> occurs concertedly, include a partially broken incoming-water O–H bond and a partially formed relay O–H bond; that is a solvent-assisted variant, not a different regioselectivity. The original mechanism schematic identifies these bond changes without depicting a falsely isolated free hydroxide reagent.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At <ph> 7, a <glutamate residue> is normally carboxylate. **Its most natural role is general-base activation of the attacking <water>.** Draw the carboxylate <oxygen> <lone pair> toward a <water> H, the <water> O–H pair toward its <oxygen>, and that <oxygen>'s <lone pair> toward C3 while the conjugated pi system moves toward the carbonyl <oxygen>:
$$
\mathrm{Glu{-}CO_2^-\cdots H{-}OH\cdots C3}
\longrightarrow
\mathrm{Glu{-}CO_2H}+\text{bound beta-hydroxy enolate}.
$$
This can be concerted, avoiding a freely diffusing hydroxide intermediate. The protonated glutamate subsequently returns its <proton> to the <enolate> system, directly or through solvent, regenerating carboxylate. A favorable arrangement is needed; ordinary carboxylate does not quantitatively deprotonate bulk <water> in a prior equilibrium.

The side-chain amide of <glutamine> is neutral. **Its N–H can donate a <hydrogen bond> to the carbonyl <oxygen> and stabilize developing <enolate> charge**, while its amide <oxygen> can accept a <hydrogen bond> to orient attacking <water>. Draw $\mathrm{Gln{-}CONH_2\cdots O{=}C1{-}C2{=}C3}$, with the dotted interaction from amide N–H to substrate <oxygen>. This assists polarization and <transition state stabilization>; the amide <nitrogen> is not an ordinary amine base because its <lone pair> is delocalized into its own carbonyl.

The guanidinium group of <arginine> is positively charged. **It can stabilize developing negative charge by hydrogen bonding and electrostatic attraction**, drawn $\mathrm{Arg{-}guanidinium^+\cdots O^{\delta-}{-}C1}$. Its delocalized cation is not the preferred neutral general base at <ph> 7. Productive <catalysis> requires stronger stabilization of the reacting arrangement than of the unreactive ground-state complex.

<Phenylalanine> and <leucine> have hydrocarbon side chains and provide no conventional acid/base or hydrogen-bond donor/acceptor group here. They can position the <enone>, exclude unproductive solvent and select the attacked face through <hydrophobic effect> and <steric hindrance>. Those binding effects can assist <catalysis> but should not be drawn as an imaginary side-chain <proton> relay. The original schematic depicts the Glu <water> relay and the Gln/Arg oxyanion-stabilizing contacts.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The beta-carbon bond-forming <transition state> retains the same basic connectivity across this <ph> range, but the predominant proton-transfer route and enzyme <protonation> change. Near the acidic end, **protonated glutamate can act as a general acid**, donating a <proton> or a strong <hydrogen bond> to carbonyl <oxygen> and making C3 more <electrophilic>; it is less available as the carboxylate general base that activates <water>. Above its local $pK_a$, **carboxylate-mediated <water> activation is favored**. Acid <catalysis> therefore shifts positive-charge stabilization toward a protonated carbonyl, whereas base-assisted attack reduces positive-charge buildup on incoming <water>. Neither a unique transition-state geometry nor a numerical <ph> optimum follows from <ph> alone.

Using a free-solution glutamate side-chain $pK_a\simeq4.2$ only as an illustration, the deprotonated fraction is $[1+10^{pK_a-\mathrm{pH}}]^{-1}$: about 0.059 at <ph> 3 and 0.998 at <ph> 7. An enzyme environment can shift that $pK_a$ substantially. The <glutamine> amide and the hydrocarbon side chains remain in their ordinary forms from <ph> 3 to 10; <arginine> guanidinium, with a typical free side-chain $pK_a$ around 12.5, remains predominantly positive even at <ph> 10. Thus the major side-chain switch among those listed is glutamate. At the alkaline end, bulk hydroxide can also contribute to nonenzymatic <conjugate addition>. A measured pH-rate profile must control substrate stability and enzyme folding before assigning every loss of activity to an active-site titration.

