= Solution
{parent=iii}

**Hydride is delivered to steroid C5 and a <proton> to C4; the C3 ketone is retained.** Use the conjugated fragment $\mathrm{O{=}C3{-}C4{=}C5}$. Carbonyl <hydrogen bonds> from Tyr91–OH and protonated Glu57 lower the barrier to <conjugate reduction>. Draw a curved arrow from the nicotinamide C4–H bond of <NADPH> to steroid C5, shift the C4=C5 pi pair toward C3–C4, and shift C3=O pi <electrons> onto <oxygen>. The nicotinamide ring rearomatizes to <NADP+>; the steroid becomes a bound <enolate>, or its protonated <enol> equivalent. <electron> shifts across the nicotinamide ring must accompany C–H bond cleavage rather than leaving an unexplained neutral <carbon> radical.

Next, reform C3=O and protonate steroid C4 from an appropriately positioned acid, directly or through an <enol>/proton-relay route. This regenerates the catalytic acid after solvent reprotonation. The enzyme fixes the hydride-accepting face so that the new C5–H has **the 5α configuration**, giving <dihydrotestosterone>; delivery to the opposite face would give a different ring-junction configuration. This is not simple 1,2 reduction of the C3 carbonyl to an alcohol. The net local bookkeeping is one hydride plus one <proton> across C4=C5, with two <electrons> supplied by <NADPH>. The original reactive-fragment diagram shows the enolate-forming arrows and the separate protonation step.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**<finasteride> is reduced at C1, leaving a C2 <enolate> that attacks nicotinamide C4 to make the covalent C–C adduct.** In the inhibitor's unsaturated lactam, the reactive chain is $\mathrm{O{=}C3{-}C2{=}C1}$, with N at steroid position 4. Activate the C3 carbonyl by hydrogen bonding; draw the reduced cofactor's C4–H bond toward inhibitor C1, the C1=C2 pi pair toward C2–C3, and the C3=O pi pair toward <oxygen>. This gives <NADP+> next to the C2-centered resonance form of the inhibitor <enolate>.

Instead of completing reduction by protonating C2, move the C2 <lone pair> toward the electrophilic C4 of the oxidized nicotinamide ring. Shift the pyridinium pi system toward its positively charged N, and reform the inhibitor carbonyl if using the oxygen-centered <enolate> representation. The result is the **C2(inhibitor)–C4(nicotinamide) bond** and a neutral dihydronicotinamide ring. Numbering the two molecules separately prevents confusing cofactor C4 with the inhibitor's N4. The lactam N–H remains present and can help position the inhibitor through hydrogen bonding; it does not form a covalent bond to the protein.

This is <mechanism-based inhibition>: enzyme chemistry generates the species that traps its own oxidized cofactor. Isolation of a cofactor–inhibitor adduct after protein denaturation, rather than persistent attachment to a denatured protein, distinguishes it from covalent modification of an amino acid side chain. The original mechanistic study combined radiolabel recovery, purification and spectroscopy to establish the adduct; its slow breakdown also cautions against treating “irreversible” as an absolute statement for every timescale.<Mechanism-Based Inhibition of Human Steroid 5α-Reductase by Finasteride: Enzyme-Catalyzed Formation of NADP–Dihydrofinasteride, a Potent Bisubstrate Analog Inhibitor by Herbert G. Bull and others>[1996, pp. 2361–2363, adduct isolation, structural analysis and Fig. 7]{ref}
